.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-webhooks-disable - Disable a webhook


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 webhooks disable  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Stop deliveries without deleting the webhook.

.PP
Reversible with 'enable', and it keeps the subscription, its id and its secret —
which is what makes it the right tool for a receiver that is down for
maintenance. 'delete' is the permanent form and loses the secret with it.

.PP
Note this is only ONE of the two ways a webhook goes inactive; see the circuit
breaker under 'rail0 webhooks --help'.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for disable


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 webhooks disable whk_abc123
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-webhooks(1)\fP
